  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12">
    <header class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500 mb-3">💰 Travel Budget</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">Trip to China Cost 2026: Complete Budget Guide</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-6">How much does a trip to China cost in 2026? A complete budget guide covering flights, hotels, food, transport, and attractions.</p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <span>📅 Last updated: August 2026</span>
        <span>⏱️ 9 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

<div class="bg-gradient-to-r from-red-50 to-orange-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-2xl p-8 mb-12 not-prose">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-red-900 mb-4">⚡ How Much Does a Trip to China Cost in 2026?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> A 10-day trip to China runs roughly <strong>¥14,400-¥30,000 ($2,000-$4,200) per person, all in</strong>. The wide range reflects three things: where you fly from, the season you travel in, and how you sleep. Round-trip airfare from North America swings from ¥5,000 to ¥15,000 depending on the month, and is the single biggest variable. Mid-range travelers can budget <strong>¥720-¥1,080 ($100-$150) a day</strong> once on the ground; budget travelers scrape by on <strong>¥360 ($50)</strong>; comfortable travelers with five-star hotels and private drivers spend <strong>¥1,440+ ($200+)</strong> a day. The two biggest peak-season offenders are <strong>Lunar New Year</strong> (late January or early February) and <strong>National Day Golden Week</strong> (October 1–7), when most Chinese people travel. Summer (June–August) and May Day (May 1–5) also push prices up. Aim for shoulder months — March, April (outside May Day), September, and November — to catch the best fares.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Here's the real breakdown from a 10-day trip I planned for a friend from New York — three cities, about ¥15,200 total, right in the middle of the mid-range bracket. The biggest surprise? How little she spent on food, even at decent restaurants.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="how-much-are-flights-to-china" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much are flights to China?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Flights are the biggest line item in the whole trip — and the one that swings the most. Here's roughly what a round-trip looks like from the main departure regions.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6"><strong>Flight costs by region (¥ and USD, 2026)</strong></p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white"><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Route</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Low Season (¥ CNY)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Low Season ($ USD)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Peak Season (¥ CNY)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Peak Season ($ USD)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">US West Coast</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥5,040-¥7,200</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$700-$1,000</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥8,640-¥12,960</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$1,200-$1,800</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">US East Coast</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥6,480-¥9,360</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$900-$1,300</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥10,800-¥15,840</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$1,500-$2,200</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">UK / Europe</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥4,320-¥6,480</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$600-$900</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥7,200-¥11,520</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$1,000-$1,600</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Southeast Asia</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥1,440-¥2,880</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$200-$400</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥2,880-¥5,040</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$400-$700</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Australia</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥3,600-¥5,760</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$500-$800</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥6,480-¥10,080</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$900-$1,400</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-gray-200 pl-5 py-2 my-8 text-gray-600 not-prose">These are estimated ranges based on typical seasonal trends. Check <a href="https://www.trip.com/t/yxIuEHgWsV2" class="text-blue-600 underline decoration-blue-300 hover:decoration-blue-600">Trip.com</a> or <a href="https://www.skyscanner.com/" class="text-blue-600 underline decoration-blue-300 hover:decoration-blue-600">Skyscanner</a> for your specific travel dates — prices shift constantly.</blockquote>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Flights are cheapest from October to March (excluding Chinese New Year). Book at least 6 weeks ahead for the best prices.</p>

<h2 id="how-much-should-i-budget-for-hotels" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much should I budget for hotels?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">After the flight, the roof over your head is the next big number. It varies more than you'd think depending on where you aim, so here's the whole range in one glance.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6"><strong>Accommodation costs (per night, single occupancy, 2026)</strong></p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white"><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Level</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Price (¥ CNY)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Price ($ USD)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">What You Get</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Budget hostel</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥58-¥108</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$8-$15</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Dorm bed, shared bathroom, basic WiFi</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Budget private room</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥144-¥288</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$20-$40</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Local chain hotel (Hanting, Home Inn), clean and basic</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Mid-range</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥360-¥648</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$50-$90</td><td class="px-6 py-4">4-star, good location, breakfast included</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Upscale</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥720-¥1,440</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$100-$200</td><td class="px-6 py-4">5-star international chain (Marriott, Hilton)</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Luxury</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥1,440+</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$200+</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Top-tier boutique or international luxury</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">A practical note: Chinese hotel chains like Hanting and Home Inn are consistently clean at roughly half the price of Western chains.</p>

<h2 id="how-much-does-food-cost-in-china" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much does food cost in China?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">And now the good part — food. This is the line item where China quietly over-delivers at every level, and here's what each tier actually buys you in a day.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12 not-prose">
      <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse bg-white rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-gray-900 text-white"><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Level</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Cost (¥ CNY)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">Cost ($ USD)</th><th class="px-6 py-4 font-bold">What That Buys You</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Street food / noodle shops</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥36-¥72</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$5-$10</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Jianbing for breakfast, noodles for lunch, dumplings for dinner</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Casual restaurants</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥72-¥144</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$10-$20</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Sit-down meals with a few dishes, local beer</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">Mid-range dining</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥144-¥288</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$20-$40</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Nice restaurant with drinks, Peking duck dinner</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-gray-100 bg-gray-50"><td class="px-6 py-4 font-semibold">High-end</td><td class="px-6 py-4">¥288+</td><td class="px-6 py-4">$40+</td><td class="px-6 py-4">Fine dining, imported wine, hotel restaurants</td></tr></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Street food in China hits differently than in the West. A proper bowl of Lanzhou beef noodles costs about ¥15-¥20 ($2-$3) and generous portions. A full Peking duck dinner for two at a mid-range Beijing restaurant runs about ¥200-¥300 ($28-$42). The value is incredible by Western standards.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">*The night market is the one thing I always tell visiting friends not to skip — it's where locals actually eat, portions are cheap, and the variety is huge: jianbing (savory crepes), pan-fried dumplings, lamb skewers, stinky tofu, candied hawthorn sticks. A proper evening of grazing — three or four different things — runs about ¥50-100. This is the food that doesn't make it into guidebooks.</p>

<h2 id="how-much-does-getting-around-china-cost" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much does getting around China cost?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Getting around China is easier and cheaper than most first-time visitors expect — the rail network does the heavy lifting between cities, and In major cities such as Beijing and Shanghai, subways can directly take you to tourist attractions, while the remaining cities are covered by buses. The decisions below are the ones that actually matter.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">High-speed rail is the backbone of travel. A good rule of thumb: second-class G-train tickets run about ¥0.45-¥0.55 per kilometer.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6"><strong>Rail vs flight — when to pick which:</strong></p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-8"><li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>Under 500 km</strong> — take the train. If the time for airport check-in and security check boarding is included, the high-speed rail, which requires no check-in and only needs a 15-minute ticket check in advance, is obviously more time-saving. For example, a Shanghai-Hangzhou ride takes about an hour and costs ¥50-¥100 ($7-$14).</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>500-1,000 km</strong> — it depends. A G-train from Beijing-Xi'an runs ¥400-¥600 ($56-$83) and takes 4-6 hours. A flight is 2 hours, but prices vary wildly — off-season airfare can actually be cheaper than the train. Check both.</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>Over 1,000 km</strong> — flights usually win on time. Beijing-Guangzhou by train is 8+ hours and ¥800+ ($111+). The same flight is 3 hours and sometimes under ¥500 ($70) off-season. But there is one catch worth mentioning...</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>Do the high-speed rail at least once.</strong> It is genuinely impressive — 300 km/h, smooth as glass, and you get to watch China's landscape roll past your window. Even on a long route, consider taking the daytime train for the experience. Book on <a href="https://www.12306.cn" class="text-blue-600 underline decoration-blue-300 hover:decoration-blue-600">12306.cn</a> or <a href="https://www.trip.com/t/nUumZWrKUV2" class="text-blue-600 underline decoration-blue-300 hover:decoration-blue-600">Trip.com</a> (English-friendly).</li></ul>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Subway fares in major cities run ¥3-¥10 ($0.40-$1.40) per ride. Taxis start at around ¥10-¥14 ($1.40-$2.00). Didi (China's Uber) is usually cheaper than a street taxi, but you have to wait for it to come from elsewhere to pick you up, and it can't stop at a wave of the hand.</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-gray-200 pl-5 py-2 my-8 text-gray-600 not-prose"><strong>Ready to book?</strong> Search train tickets directly on Trip.com — enter your cities and dates below. <iframe border="0" src="https://www.trip.com/partners/ad/S18626540?Allianceid=9227653&SID=323956697&trip_sub1=" style="width:320px;height:480px;max-width:100%" frameborder="0" scrolling="no" id="S18626540" loading="lazy"></iframe> 🚄 <a href="https://www.trip.com/t/nUumZWrKUV2" class="text-blue-600 underline decoration-blue-300 hover:decoration-blue-600">Search train tickets on Trip.com</a></blockquote>

<h2 id="how-much-are-attraction-entry-fees" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much are attraction entry fees?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">Entry fees are the easiest part of the budget — most attractions are sold individually, but the tourism cities also bundle popular sights into multi-attraction passes that often save 20–30%. The trick is to plan your sightseeing route around what the pass actually covers, not the other way around. Not sure how to put it together? Drop me a line — I help visitors plan routes like this as part of my own trip research.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">China's attractions are startlingly cheap by Western standards. Most temples, museums, and historical sites charge ¥15-¥120 ($2-$17) for entry. The Forbidden City costs ¥60 ($8) in peak season. The Terracotta Warriors are ¥120 ($17). Even the best sights rarely hit ¥200 ($28).</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">That said, your daily attraction spend varies a lot depending on what you visit:</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mb-8"><li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>One cheap site</strong> (a temple, a park, a lesser museum): ¥0-¥45 ($0-$6)</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>One major site</strong> (Forbidden City, Terracotta Warriors, Great Wall entry): ¥45-¥120 ($6-$17)</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>Two major sites in a day</strong> (common in Beijing or Xi'an): ¥80-¥200 ($11-$28)</li>
<li class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700"><strong>Adding extras</strong> (audio guides ¥20-¥40 each, cable cars ¥100-¥200, private guides ¥200-¥500): can easily double the cost</li></ul>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">For context, the Great Wall's entry is only ¥45 ($6), but the round-trip cable car adds ¥120 ($17). The Forbidden City's ¥60 ($8) covers the grounds, but the treasure gallery and clock gallery are extra. Budget accordingly.</p>

<h2 id="how-much-does-a-full-trip-cost-in-total" class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-6 mt-14">How much does a full trip cost in total?</h2>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">So what does it all add up to? Here's how a 10-day trip looks at three different spending levels.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6">For a typical 10-day, three-city first-timer itinerary, tuned to Trip.com's 2026 reference rates.</p>
<p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-gray-700 mb-6"><strong>Budget Traveler (10 days, shoulder season)</strong> — ¥12,000-¥15,500 ($1,700-$2,150)</p>
